  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 105 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCool, light northerly flow will remain over the area for Friday and \nFriday night with clear skies, and even drier air spread in from the \nnorth. There is even higher confidence in a widespread hard freeze \nacross the plains with a 40-50% chance of seeing temperatures dip \nbelow 28 degrees.  Have issued a new Freeze Watch for all of the \nsoutheast plains Friday Night into early Saturday morning.  It is \npossible that lee troughing along the mountains Friday night could \nshift winds from the west of I-25...keeping temperatures from \ndipping below freezing.  Areas where this could occur would be \neastern Fremont county and western Huerfano county...however it will \nbe very close and have opted to include them in the Freeze Watch but \nits possible these areas could end up with a frost or light freeze \ninstead.  \n\nTemperatures will rebound over the weekend with temperatures \nclimbing back to near to a little above normal by Sunday.  Closed \nlow spinning off the southwest coast of CA will fill and kick out \nacross the Desert Southwest and across CO late weekend into early \nnext week.  This could produce a some isolated showers over the \nmountains, but any precipitation will be spotty and light with near \nnormal temperatures continuing across the plains.  \n\nWarmer temperatures will return for the middle of next week as \nsouthwesterly flow increases ahead of the next west coast system \nwhich could bring impacts to the central Rockies late week. -KT",
